var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/#reference","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/#Contents","page":"Reference","text":"Pages = [\"95-reference.md\"]","title":"Contents"},{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/#Index","page":"Reference","text":"Pages = [\"95-reference.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"constant","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules._ASSOCIATIVE_SYMBOLS","page":"Reference","text":"_ASSOCIATIVE_SYMBOLS\n\nOpenMath symbols denoting associative operations.  An n-ary OSR expression with such a head is normalized to left-associated binary SymbolicUtils terms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules._ASSOCIATIVE_SYMBOLS"},{"category":"constant","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules._BINDER_HEADS","page":"Reference","text":"_BINDER_HEADS\n\nCanonical OSR heads that introduce a lexical scope.  Lambda binds one variable, Forall and Exists bind a vector of variable names.  Every other binding construct — an integral, a sum, a product, a derivative — carries its bound variable inside a Lambda, as the OpenMath fns1#lambda symbol prescribes, so it needs no separate entry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules._BINDER_HEADS"},{"category":"constant","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules._CANONICAL_ARITHMETIC","page":"Reference","text":"_CANONICAL_ARITHMETIC\n\nJulia operation behind every canonical OSR arithmetic head.  A rule file that renames those heads keeps working, but its expressions are then opaque to numeric folding and every guarded rewrite stays conservative.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules._CANONICAL_ARITHMETIC"},{"category":"constant","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules._COMMUTATIVE_SYMBOLS","page":"Reference","text":"_COMMUTATIVE_SYMBOLS\n\nOpenMath symbols denoting operations whose operands may be reordered.  Patterns headed by one of these compile to a SymbolicUtils.ACRule, so a single rule matches every operand order instead of requiring a mirrored copy per order.\n\nopenmath:arith1#times is deliberately absent.  An OSR expression carries no shape information, so a times operand may be a matrix or a tensor and reordering it would be unsound.  The same applies to tensor products, contractions, and axis permutations, which must use dedicated heads.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules._COMMUTATIVE_SYMBOLS"},{"category":"constant","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules._DEFAULT_SEMANTICS","page":"Reference","text":"_DEFAULT_SEMANTICS\n\nOpenMath Content Dictionary symbol of every canonical OSR head.  A rule file declares its own semantics block, and that declaration always wins; this map only resolves heads used outside a rule file, for example when osr_to_expr is called directly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules._DEFAULT_SEMANTICS"},{"category":"constant","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules._NUMERIC_CONDITIONS","page":"Reference","text":"_NUMERIC_CONDITIONS\n\nPredicate deciding a relation head, used only when every operand is a closed arithmetic expression.  For a symbolic operand these predicates answer \"not proved\", which is not the same as \"false\", so they may not decide a condition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules._NUMERIC_CONDITIONS"},{"category":"constant","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules._OSR_PREDICATES","page":"Reference","text":"_OSR_PREDICATES\n\nJulia function implementing each OSR constraint predicate.  A predicate that is not listed here is resolved in the module that loads the rule file, so a host can supply its own without changing this library.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules._OSR_PREDICATES"},{"category":"constant","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules._PROPERTIES","page":"Reference","text":"_PROPERTIES\n\nThe properties a hypothesis can establish about a term.  check_assumption answers each one by entailment, so a hypothesis proves every property its domain implies rather than only the one it was written as.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules._PROPERTIES"},{"category":"constant","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules._WILDCARD_DOMAINS","page":"Reference","text":"_WILDCARD_DOMAINS\n\nPredicate guarding a typed blank such as m_integer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules._WILDCARD_DOMAINS"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.ElementOf","page":"Reference","text":"ElementOf(var, domain)\n\nThe OSR form of a membership hypothesis: var lies in domain, which may be a DomainSets.jl domain, an IntervalSets.jl interval, or a Julia type.  ∈ builds one for a symbolic term, and the domain predicates read it through entailed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.ElementOf"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.OSRDispatch","page":"Reference","text":"OSRDispatch(rules)\n\nA rewriter that applies rules to a term the way SymbolicUtils.Rewriters.Chain does — each rule in turn, threading the result — while skipping the rules whose pattern is headed by a different operation.\n\nSelecting candidates costs one dictionary lookup instead of one matcher call per rule, which is what keeps a large rule set, such as the 6000 RUBI integration rules, usable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.OSRDispatch"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.OSRInference","page":"Reference","text":"OSRInference\n\nOne multi-premise inference declared by an Open Symbolic Rules inference manifest.  Values remain JSON-compatible; applying an inference is the responsibility of the host proof engine.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.OSRInference"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.OSRPiece","page":"Reference","text":"OSRPiece(value, condition)\n\nOne branch of a Piecewise expression.  A final Otherwise branch has no condition of its own, so its condition is nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.OSRPiece"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.OSRProof","page":"Reference","text":"OSRProof\n\nThe outcome of an equivalence check.  When verified, the two sides rewrite to a common normal_form along left_steps and right_steps, under assumptions.\n\nEvery OSR rule is an oriented instance of an equation, so the steps on the right read backwards give a derivation of right from normal_form, and the two chains together form a derivation of left = right.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.OSRProof"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.OSRProofStep","page":"Reference","text":"OSRProofStep(rule, before, after)\n\nOne licensed rewrite: rule turned the subterm before into after.  A step is local, so it justifies the whole expression only together with congruence — which is exactly how a rewrite tactic works in a proof assistant.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.OSRProofStep"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.OSRRule","page":"Reference","text":"OSRRule(name, description, provenance, rule)\n\nA callable SymbolicUtils rewrite rule annotated with its stable OSR identity:id and optional machine-readable provenance from the source rule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.OSRRule"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.SparsePolynomial","page":"Reference","text":"Exact sparse multivariate polynomial primitives for algebraic backends.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.SparsePolynomial"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.AtomQ-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"AtomQ(u)\n\nReturn whether u has no arguments of its own.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.AtomQ"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.EqQ-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"EqQ(u, v)\n\nReturn whether u and v are provably equal.  Two closed arithmetic expressions are compared by value and two identical terms are equal by construction; nothing else counts as a proof, so an unproved equality is reported as false and the guarded rewrite is skipped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.EqQ"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.FalseQ-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"FalseQ(u)\n\nReturn whether u is literally the Boolean false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.FalseQ"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.FractionQ-Tuple","page":"Reference","text":"FractionQ(us...)\n\nReturn whether every argument is an exact rational number that is not an integer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.FractionQ"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.FreeQ-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"FreeQ(expr, var)\n\nReturns true if expr has no free occurrence of var.  Equivalent to Mathematica's FreeQ, and used primarily to identify constants relative to an integration or differentiation variable.\n\nBinders are respected: an occurrence bound by an enclosing Lambda, Forall, or Exists is not an occurrence of the free variable, so Lambda(x, Sin(x)) is free of x.\n\nEither side may be a collection.  A quantifier binds a list, so var may be the whole binder and the predicate then asks about every variable it declares; and RUBI writes FreeQ[{a, b, m}, x], so expr may be a list whose every element must be free of the variable.  When var is neither a variable nor a collection, expr is searched for it structurally instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.FreeQ"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.GeQ-Tuple","page":"Reference","text":"GeQ(u, v)\nGeQ(u, v, w)\n\nReturn whether the real values of the arguments are non-increasing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.GeQ"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.GtQ-Tuple","page":"Reference","text":"GtQ(u, v)\nGtQ(u, v, w)\n\nReturn whether the real values of the arguments are strictly decreasing, so GtQ(u, v, w) means u > v > w.  Arguments that are not closed real expressions make the predicate false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.GtQ"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.HalfIntegerQ-Tuple","page":"Reference","text":"HalfIntegerQ(us...)\n\nReturn whether every argument is an odd multiple of one half.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.HalfIntegerQ"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.IGeQ-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"IGeQ(u, n)\n\nReturn whether u is an integer greater than or equal to n.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.IGeQ"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.IGtQ-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"IGtQ(u, n)\n\nReturn whether u is an integer strictly greater than n.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.IGtQ"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.ILeQ-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"ILeQ(u, n)\n\nReturn whether u is an integer less than or equal to n.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.ILeQ"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.ILtQ-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"ILtQ(u, n)\n\nReturn whether u is an integer strictly less than n.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.ILtQ"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.IntegerQ-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"IntegerQ(u)\n\nReturn whether u is provably an integer, either by value or by hypothesis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.IntegerQ"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.IntegersQ-Tuple","page":"Reference","text":"IntegersQ(us...)\n\nReturn whether every argument is provably an integer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.IntegersQ"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.LeQ-Tuple","page":"Reference","text":"LeQ(u, v)\nLeQ(u, v, w)\n\nReturn whether the real values of the arguments are non-decreasing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.LeQ"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.LinearQ-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"LinearQ(u, x)\n\nReturn whether u is a polynomial of degree one in x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.LinearQ"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.LtQ-Tuple","page":"Reference","text":"LtQ(u, v)\nLtQ(u, v, w)\n\nReturn whether the real values of the arguments are strictly increasing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.LtQ"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.MemberQ-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"MemberQ(collection, u)\n\nReturn whether u occurs in collection, which may be a Julia collection or a canonical List term.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.MemberQ"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.NeQ-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"NeQ(u, v)\n\nReturn whether u and v are provably different.  Symbolic terms are not unequal merely because their structures differ: without a proof the predicate returns false and the guarded rewrite is skipped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.NeQ"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.NegQ-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"NegQ(u)\n\nReturn whether u is provably negative, either by value or by hypothesis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.NegQ"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.NotEqual-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"NotEqual(a, b)\n\nPredicate to check whether an inequality is established.  This is the OSR spelling of RUBI's NeQ and shares its conservative semantics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.NotEqual"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.PolyQ-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"PolyQ(u, x)\nPolyQ(u, x, n)\n\nReturn whether u is a polynomial in x, optionally of degree exactly n.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.PolyQ"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.PolynomialQ-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"PolynomialQ(u, x)\n\nReturn whether u is a polynomial in x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.PolynomialQ"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.PosQ-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"PosQ(u)\n\nReturn whether u is provably positive, either by value or by hypothesis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.PosQ"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.PowerQ-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"PowerQ(u)\n\nReturn whether u is a canonical Power term.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.PowerQ"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.ProductQ-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"ProductQ(u)\n\nReturn whether u is a canonical Multiply term.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.ProductQ"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.QuadraticQ-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"QuadraticQ(u, x)\n\nReturn whether u is a polynomial of degree two in x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.QuadraticQ"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.RationalQ-Tuple","page":"Reference","text":"RationalQ(us...)\n\nReturn whether every argument is an exact rational number.  A floating-point value is not exact and is therefore rejected.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.RationalQ"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.SumQ-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"SumQ(u)\n\nReturn whether u is a canonical Add term.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.SumQ"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules._alpha_rename-Tuple{Any, AbstractDict{Symbol, Symbol}}","page":"Reference","text":"_alpha_rename(expr, renamings)\n\nRename the variables of a binder term according to renamings, a map from the current name to the new one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules._alpha_rename"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules._collect_constraint_operators!-Tuple{Set{String}, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"_collect_constraint_operators!(operators, constraint)\n\nCollect the mathematical operators a constraint applies its predicates to.  A predicate name is not a mathematical operator and needs no OpenMath binding, and Not, And, and Or nest constraints rather than expressions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules._collect_constraint_operators!"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules._compile_constraint-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"_compile_constraint(constraint, semantics)\n\nCompile one OSR constraint into a Julia expression that evaluates to a Bool. Not, And, and Or are constraint combinators: they nest constraints and compile to Julia control flow, never to a symbolic logic term.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules._compile_constraint"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules._domain_entails-Tuple{Any, Symbol}","page":"Reference","text":"_domain_entails(domain, property)\n\nReturn whether membership of domain proves property.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules._domain_entails"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules._equality_parent-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:EqualityConstraint}}","page":"Reference","text":"equality_satisfiable(constraints) -> Bool\n\nDecide conjunctions of ground equality and disequality constraints over uninterpreted symbols. Equalities are closed transitively with a pure-Julia union-find structure before all disequalities are checked.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules._equality_parent"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules._exact_power-Tuple{Number, Number}","page":"Reference","text":"_exact_power(base, exponent)\n\nRaise base to exponent without losing exactness.  Julia refuses to raise an integer to a negative integer power, yet Power(u, -1) is how OSR spells a reciprocal, so the base is widened to a rational first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules._exact_power"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules._fact_entails-Tuple{Any, Any, Symbol}","page":"Reference","text":"_fact_entails(fact, subject, property)\n\nReturn whether one hypothesis proves property of subject.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules._fact_entails"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules._fresh_name-Tuple{Symbol, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"_fresh_name(base, taken)\n\nReturn a variant of base that is not in taken.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules._fresh_name"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules._interval_entails-Tuple{Any, Symbol}","page":"Reference","text":"_interval_entails(domain, property)\n\nEntailment of an ordered domain, read from its bounds.  A bound at zero only excludes zero when it is open, so [0, ∞) is not a proof of positivity while (0, ∞) is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules._interval_entails"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules._literal-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"_literal(expr)\n\nUnwrap a symbolic numeric literal.  SymbolicUtils wraps the arguments of a term, so arguments(Add(1, 2)) yields symbolic constants rather than Ints.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules._literal"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules._nonempty_match-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"_nonempty_match(matched)\n\nReturn whether a sequence wildcard bound at least one expression.  OSR's __ spelling requires a non-empty match, whereas ___ accepts an empty one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules._nonempty_match"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules._openmath_symbol-Tuple{AbstractString, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"_openmath_symbol(head, semantics)\n\nReturn the OpenMath symbol bound to head, or nothing when the head has no declared and no canonical binding.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules._openmath_symbol"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules._relation_entails-Tuple{Symbol, Any, Symbol}","page":"Reference","text":"_relation_entails(head, operands, property)\n\nEntailment of a hypothesis written as a relation, such as GreaterThan(x, 0). A comparison also orders its subject, so it proves that the subject is real.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules._relation_entails"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules._rule_macro-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"_rule_macro(pattern, semantics)\n\nReturn the SymbolicUtils rule macro a pattern must be compiled with.  A pattern headed by a commutative operation becomes an @acrule, which matches every operand order with a single rule instead of one rule per order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules._rule_macro"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules._type_entails-Tuple{Type, Symbol}","page":"Reference","text":"_type_entails(type, property)\n\nEntailment of a hypothesis written as a Julia type, as in x ∈ Int.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules._type_entails"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules._variable_name-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"_variable_name(value)\n\nReturn the name of a variable, whether it is written as a Julia Symbol — the form quantifier binders use — or as a SymbolicUtils symbol.  Anything else has no name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules._variable_name"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules._wildcard_to_expr-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Reference","text":"_wildcard_to_expr(node; reference=false)\n\nCompile an OSR v0.1 wildcard spelling into a SymbolicUtils matcher pattern, or return nothing when node names an ordinary symbol.  A reference wildcard stands for a binding the pattern already made — as in a rule's result or in a constraint argument — so it carries no predicate of its own.\n\nspelling meaning pattern\n~x slot ~x\nx_ blank ~x\nx_<domain> typed blank ~x::<predicate>\nxs__ sequence, at least one ~~xs::_nonempty_match\nxs___ sequence, possibly empty ~~xs\n\nOSR also spells an optional operand a., which needs a matcher that knows the identity element of the enclosing operation.  SymbolicUtils offers that only for the native +, *, and ^, never for the uninterpreted heads an OSR rule file declares, so such a wildcard is rejected instead of being compiled into a rule that could never fire.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules._wildcard_to_expr"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.alpha_equivalent-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"alpha_equivalent(left, right)\n\nReturn whether two expressions are equal up to a consistent renaming of their bound variables.  Free variables are compared by name.\n\njulia> using OpenSymbolicRules, SymbolicUtils\n\njulia> @syms x y;\n\njulia> OpenSymbolicRules.alpha_equivalent(Lambda(x, Sin(x)), Lambda(y, Sin(y)))\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.alpha_equivalent"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.assuming-Tuple{Any, Vararg{Any}}","page":"Reference","text":"assuming(f, assumptions...)\n\nExecutes a function f within a context where the provided assumptions hold true. Useful for providing local hypotheses to the CAS.\n\nExample:\n\nassuming(x > 0) do\n    simplify(Abs(x), rules)\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.assuming"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.binder_body-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"binder_body(expr)\n\nReturn the body a binder term scopes over.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.binder_body"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.bound_variables-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"bound_variables(expr)\n\nReturn the variable names the binder term expr introduces, or nothing when expr binds nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.bound_variables"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.build_simplifier-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"Reference","text":"build_simplifier(rules::AbstractVector)\n\nBuilds a fixed-point bottom-up rewriter from a vector of SymbolicUtils rules. Rules are applied through an OSRDispatch, which skips the rules whose pattern is headed by a different operation than the term at hand.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.build_simplifier"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.candidate_positions-Tuple{OSRDispatch, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"candidate_positions(dispatch, expr)\n\nReturn the positions, in the original rule order, of the rules that can match expr.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.candidate_positions"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.check_assumption-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"check_assumption(predicate)\n\nChecks whether predicate is one of the hypotheses in scope, comparing it literally.  The domain predicates use entailed instead, which also accepts a hypothesis that merely implies the property.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.check_assumption"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.decide_condition-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"decide_condition(condition)\n\nDecide a Piecewise condition, returning true, false, or nothing when it is undecided.\n\nA condition is decided when it is a Boolean literal, when it is a relation over closed arithmetic expressions, or when it — or its negation — is an active hypothesis.  And, Or, and Not combine those answers with three-valued logic, so a conjunction with one false operand is false even when the other is unknown.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.decide_condition"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.entailed-Tuple{Any, Symbol}","page":"Reference","text":"entailed(subject, property)\n\nReturn whether the hypotheses in scope prove property of subject.\n\nEach hypothesis is asked on its own, so the answer is sound but does not combine two hypotheses into a third: x ∈ ℤ proves integrality and x > 0 proves positivity, yet neither alone proves that x is a positive integer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.entailed"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.equality_model-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:EqualityConstraint}}","page":"Reference","text":"equality_model(constraints) -> Union{Dict{Symbol,Symbol}, Nothing}\n\nReturn the equivalence-class representative for every term occurring in a satisfiable ground equality problem, or nothing if its disequalities contradict the equality closure. The returned map is a checkable model for the equality theory: equal terms have equal representatives.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.equality_model"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.free_variables-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"free_variables(expr)\n\nReturn the names that occur free in expr.  A name bound by an enclosing Lambda, Forall, or Exists is not free.\n\njulia> using OpenSymbolicRules, SymbolicUtils\n\njulia> @syms x y;\n\njulia> OpenSymbolicRules.free_variables(Lambda(x, Add(x, y)))\nSet{Symbol} with 1 element:\n  :y\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.free_variables"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.groebner_basis-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:SparsePolynomial}}","page":"Reference","text":"groebner_basis(generators; ordering=:grevlex)\n\nCompute a reduced Gröbner basis with Buchberger's algorithm over the rational coefficient field. This pure-Julia reference implementation prioritizes exact results and traceable invariants over large-system performance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.groebner_basis"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.is_integer-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"is_integer(x)\n\nPredicate to check if x is an integer, by value or by hypothesis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.is_integer"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.is_nonzero-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"is_nonzero(x)\n\nReturns whether x is provably nonzero.  For symbolic values, this requires an explicit IsNonzero(x) assumption or a sign assumption; an unknown value is deliberately not treated as nonzero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.is_nonzero"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.is_numeric-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"is_numeric(x)\n\nPredicate to check if x denotes a numeric constant.  A closed arithmetic expression such as Add(1, 2) is numeric; an expression containing a symbol is not.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.is_numeric"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.linear_model-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:LinearConstraint}}","page":"Reference","text":"linear_model(constraints) -> Union{Dict{Symbol,Rational{BigInt}}, Nothing}\n\nReturn an exact rational model for a satisfiable conjunction of linear constraints, or nothing if it is inconsistent. The model is reconstructed from the Fourier–Motzkin elimination layers and satisfies strict bounds without floating point approximation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.linear_model"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.linear_satisfiable-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:LinearConstraint}}","page":"Reference","text":"linear_satisfiable(constraints) -> Bool\n\nDecide conjunctions of linear constraints over exact rational arithmetic using Fourier–Motzkin elimination. A constraint represents Σ coefficients[variable] * variable relation bound, where relation is one of :le, :lt, or :eq. Strict inequalities are preserved exactly.\n\nThis is a theory solver for a future pure-Julia DPLL(T) engine; it does not use floating point arithmetic, a native library, or an external SMT process.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.linear_satisfiable"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.linear_smt_model-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:AbstractVector{<:Integer}}, AbstractDict{<:Integer, <:LinearConstraint}}","page":"Reference","text":"linear_smt_model(clauses, atoms) -> Union{NamedTuple, Nothing}\n\nReturn a checkable model for a satisfiable linear DPLL(T) problem. The result has booleans, a DIMACS-variable assignment, and rationals, the exact rational assignment for the selected linear-theory branch. Return nothing when the problem is unsatisfiable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.linear_smt_model"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.linear_smt_satisfiable-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:AbstractVector{<:Integer}}, AbstractDict{<:Integer, <:LinearConstraint}}","page":"Reference","text":"linear_smt_satisfiable(clauses, atoms) -> Bool\n\nDecide a propositional CNF formula whose atoms are exact rational linear constraints. clauses uses DIMACS literals and atoms maps each positive atom number to a LinearConstraint. The solver combines the local pure-Julia DPLL search with Fourier–Motzkin theory consistency checks.\n\nNegated equality is expanded exactly into its strict-less-than and strict-greater-than theory branches.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.linear_smt_satisfiable"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.load_inference_profile-Tuple{AbstractString, Symbol}","page":"Reference","text":"load_inference_profile(root, profile)\n\nLoad the structured multi-premise inferences declared by a named OSR inference profile.  This function does not execute inferences.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.load_inference_profile"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.normalize_fact-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"normalize_fact(fact)\n\nReturn the OSR facts a hypothesis stands for.\n\nA host may spell a membership hypothesis in its own type — Symbolics.jl builds a VarDomainPairing for x ∈ 2..5 once it is loaded — so a hypothesis is normalised to ElementOf when it enters the context, and the entailment rules then only ever see OSR facts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.normalize_fact"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.occurs_free-Tuple{Any, Symbol}","page":"Reference","text":"occurs_free(expr, name)\n\nReturn whether name occurs free in expr.  This answers the single question FreeQ asks without materialising the whole free-variable set, and it stops at the first free occurrence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.occurs_free"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.osr_collection-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"osr_collection(expr)\n\nReturn the elements of a collection expression, or nothing when expr is not one.  SymbolicUtils keeps a vector of plain values as a literal but wraps a vector holding symbolic expressions in an array_literal term, so a collection reaches this library in either shape.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.osr_collection"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.osr_degree-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"osr_degree(expr, variable)\n\nReturn the degree of expr as a polynomial in variable, or nothing when expr is not a polynomial built from canonical arithmetic heads.  A subterm free of variable is a coefficient of degree zero, whatever its own shape.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.osr_degree"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.osr_number-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"osr_number(expr)\n\nReturn the exact numeric value of a closed OSR arithmetic expression, or nothing when expr still contains a symbol, uses a head with no arithmetic meaning, or does not denote a number at all.\n\njulia> using OpenSymbolicRules\n\njulia> OpenSymbolicRules.osr_number(Power(2, -1))\n1//2\n\njulia> OpenSymbolicRules.osr_number(Add(1, 2))\n3\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.osr_number"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.osr_substitute-Tuple{Any, Pair}","page":"Reference","text":"osr_substitute(expr, variable => replacement)\n\nSubstitute replacement for the free occurrences of variable in expr.  The substitution is capture-avoiding: a binder whose variable occurs free in replacement is alpha-renamed first, so the replacement keeps referring to the same variable it did outside the binder.\n\njulia> using OpenSymbolicRules, SymbolicUtils\n\njulia> @syms x y;\n\njulia> OpenSymbolicRules.osr_substitute(Lambda(y, Add(x, y)), x => y)\nLambda(y1, Add(y, y1))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.osr_substitute"},{"category":"function","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.osr_to_expr","page":"Reference","text":"osr_to_expr(node, semantics=_DEFAULT_SEMANTICS; reference=false)\n\nRecursively parse an OSR JSON node into a Julia expression for SymbolicUtils. semantics is the rule file's head-to-OpenMath-symbol map; it decides which n-ary expressions are normalized to left-associated binary terms.  Set reference when compiling a rule's result or a constraint argument, where a wildcard refers to a binding the pattern already made.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.osr_to_expr"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.piecewise_pieces-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"piecewise_pieces(expr)\n\nReturn the branches of a Piecewise expression in declaration order, or nothing when expr is not a piecewise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.piecewise_pieces"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.prove-Tuple{Any, Any, AbstractVector}","page":"Reference","text":"prove(left, right, rules; assumptions=nothing)\nprove(equation, rules; assumptions=nothing)\n\nSearch for a rewrite path between two expressions and return the OSRProof that records it.\n\nBoth sides are normalised with rules and the normal forms are compared up to a renaming of bound variables, so the search succeeds exactly when the two sides are joinable by the rule set.  This is sound but incomplete: a rule set that is not confluent may fail to join two equivalent expressions, which is why a failure is reported as unproved rather than as a disproof.\n\n@syms x\nrules = @load_osr_profile(\"path/to/Trigonometry\")\nproof = prove(Add(Power(Sin(x), 2), Power(Cos(x), 2)), 1, rules)\nverified(proof) # true\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.prove"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.rational_assumptions_satisfiable-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"rational_assumptions_satisfiable(facts) -> Union{Bool, Nothing}\n\nCheck a supported collection of CAS hypotheses with the exact rational linear theory. true means the converted hypotheses have a rational model, false means they are contradictory, and nothing means at least one hypothesis is outside this deliberately conservative fragment. Supported facts are IsPositive(x), IsNegative(x), GreaterThan(x, c), and LessThan(x, c) for a named symbolic variable x and an exact rational constant c.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.rational_assumptions_satisfiable"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.rational_smt_model-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:AbstractVector{<:Integer}}, AbstractDict{<:Integer, <:Union{EqualityConstraint, LinearConstraint}}}","page":"Reference","text":"rational_smt_model(clauses, atoms) -> Union{NamedTuple, Nothing}\n\nReturn Boolean and exact rational witnesses for rational_smt_satisfiable. The Boolean assignments use the caller's equality/disequality atom semantics, not the internal equality normalization.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.rational_smt_model"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.rational_smt_satisfiable-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:AbstractVector{<:Integer}}, AbstractDict{<:Integer, <:Union{EqualityConstraint, LinearConstraint}}}","page":"Reference","text":"rational_smt_satisfiable(clauses, atoms) -> Bool\n\nDecide a DPLL(T) problem containing exact rational linear atoms and equality or disequality atoms over the same rational variables. Equality atoms are translated to x - y = 0; disequality atoms are translated with the exact strict-order branching already used by the linear theory. This entry point is not for uninterpreted equality terms, which belong to smt_satisfiable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.rational_smt_satisfiable"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.rule_head-Tuple{OSRRule}","page":"Reference","text":"rule_head(rule)\n\nReturn the operation a rule's pattern requires at the root of a term, or nothing when the rule may match a term of any head.\n\nA rewrite whose pattern is headed by a concrete operation cannot match a term with a different one, so its matcher never has to run.  Three kinds of pattern have no such requirement and must always be tried: a bare slot, a slot in head position, and a pattern containing an optional slot, which SymbolicUtils lets match a term that lacks the operation entirely.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.rule_head"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.rule_paths-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Reference","text":"rule_paths(root; profile=nothing)\n\nReturn the ordered rewrite manifests declared by an OSR repository or its rules directory.  With profile, return the named rewrite profile.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.rule_paths"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.sat_model-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:AbstractVector{<:Integer}}}","page":"Reference","text":"sat_model(clauses) -> Union{Dict{Int,Bool}, Nothing}\n\nReturn a Boolean model for a satisfiable DIMACS CNF formula, or nothing when it is unsatisfiable. The returned dictionary is a directly checkable witness: every input clause contains at least one literal whose variable has the stated truth value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.sat_model"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.satisfiable-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:AbstractVector{<:Integer}}}","page":"Reference","text":"satisfiable(clauses) -> Bool\n\nDecide a propositional CNF formula using a pure-Julia DPLL implementation. Each inner vector is a disjunction and the outer vector is a conjunction. Literals use the DIMACS convention: positive n denotes variable n, and negative -n denotes its negation. Literal zero is invalid.\n\nThis is deliberately a SAT foundation, not a claim to support all SMT theories. It is the Boolean kernel on which a pure-Julia DPLL(T) layer can add equality, rational arithmetic, and other theory solvers with explicit proof evidence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.satisfiable"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.select_piece-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"select_piece(expr)\n\nReduce a Piecewise expression to the value of the branch that applies, or return it unchanged when the applicable branch is not settled.\n\nBranches are examined in order.  A branch is taken once its condition is decided true and every earlier condition is decided false; an undecided condition stops the search, because a later branch may not overtake one that might yet apply.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.select_piece"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.smt_model-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:AbstractVector{<:Integer}}, AbstractDict{<:Integer, <:EqualityConstraint}}","page":"Reference","text":"smt_model(clauses, atoms) -> Union{NamedTuple, Nothing}\n\nReturn a checkable model for CNF clauses over ground equality atoms. The result contains the Boolean booleans assignment and the equality classes map, or nothing when the problem is unsatisfiable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.smt_model"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.smt_satisfiable-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:AbstractVector{<:Integer}}, AbstractDict{<:Integer, <:EqualityConstraint}}","page":"Reference","text":"smt_satisfiable(clauses, atoms) -> Bool\n\nDecide CNF Boolean clauses over ground equality atoms. Clauses use DIMACS literals and atoms maps each positive number to an EqualityConstraint. This is a pure-Julia DPLL(T) entry point for the equality theory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.smt_satisfiable"},{"category":"function","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.to_osr","page":"Reference","text":"to_osr(expr)\n\nConverts Symbolics.jl operations into OpenSymbolicRules.jl pedagogical format. Requires loading Symbolics first (using Symbolics).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.to_osr"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.to_sparse_polynomial-Tuple{Any, AbstractVector{Symbol}}","page":"Reference","text":"to_sparse_polynomial(expression, variables) -> SparsePolynomial\n\nConvert a SymbolicUtils expression to an exact polynomial over ℚ. Variables are explicitly named to prevent accidental conversion of parameters or non-polynomial subexpressions. Only addition, multiplication, non-negative integer powers, and exact rational coefficients are admitted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.to_sparse_polynomial"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.to_symbolic_polynomial-Tuple{SparsePolynomial, AbstractDict{Symbol}}","page":"Reference","text":"to_symbolic_polynomial(polynomial, variables)\n\nRebuild a SymbolicUtils expression from an exact sparse polynomial. The map must associate every polynomial variable name with its symbolic expression.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.to_symbolic_polynomial"},{"category":"function","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.to_symbolics","page":"Reference","text":"to_symbolics(expr)\n\nConverts OpenSymbolicRules.jl pedagogical format back into Symbolics.jl operations. Requires loading Symbolics first (using Symbolics).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.to_symbolics"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.verified-Tuple{OSRProof}","page":"Reference","text":"verified(proof)\n\nReturn whether a rewrite path between the two sides was found.\n\nA verified proof is sound: every step is a rule application whose constraints held where it fired.  An unverified one is not a disproof — the rule set may simply lack the path — so the two sides are reported as unproved rather than unequal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.verified"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#SymbolicUtils.simplify-Tuple{Any, AbstractVector}","page":"Reference","text":"simplify(expr, rules::AbstractVector; mode::Symbol=:fast, on_step=nothing)\n\nApplies a set of OSR rules to an expression until it stops changing. Supported modes:\n\n:fast : standard execution (default). Returns the result.\n:trace : returns a tuple (result, steps_array) where each step is a NamedTuple (rule, before, after).\n\nSet on_step to a callback to observe each rewrite without requiring output from this library.  Rules loaded by @load_osr are OSRRule values, so trace steps expose their stable name and human-readable description.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SymbolicUtils.simplify"},{"category":"macro","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.@load_osr-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"@load_osr(\"path/to/rule.json\")\n\nLoad an Open Symbolic Rules JSON file at compile-time and return a vector of SymbolicUtils.jl rules.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.@load_osr"},{"category":"macro","location":"95-reference/#OpenSymbolicRules.@load_osr_profile","page":"Reference","text":"@load_osr_profile(\"repository/path\"[, :profile])\n\nCompile the complete default or named rewrite manifest of an OSR repository. The repository path is relative to the caller source file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.@load_osr_profile"},{"category":"section","location":"10-tutorials/10-basic-usage/#Basic-Usage","page":"Basic Usage","text":"This tutorial shows how to load and apply mathematical rules.","title":"Basic Usage"},{"category":"section","location":"10-tutorials/10-basic-usage/#Loading-Rules","page":"Basic Usage","text":"Rules are stored in JSON files. You can load them at compile-time using the @load_osr macro:\n\nusing OpenSymbolicRules\n\n# Assuming you have cloned the Algebra rule repository\nalg_rules = @load_osr_profile(\"path/to/Algebra\")\n\nThe loader verifies that every mathematical operator used by the selected rule files has an openmath:<content-dictionary>#<symbol> declaration in its semantics object. Constraint predicates are intentionally exempt.","title":"Loading Rules"},{"category":"section","location":"10-tutorials/10-basic-usage/#Simplifying-Expressions","page":"Basic Usage","text":"Once loaded, you can apply rules to SymbolicUtils.jl expressions using the simplify function.\n\nusing SymbolicUtils\n@syms x Power(a, b) Multiply(a, b)\n\n# Create an expression: (x^2)^3\nexpr = Power(Power(x, 2), 3)\n\n# Simplify it\nres = simplify(expr, alg_rules)\n# -> Power(x, Multiply(2, 3))","title":"Simplifying Expressions"},{"category":"section","location":"10-tutorials/10-basic-usage/#Step-by-Step-Tracing","page":"Basic Usage","text":"For educational purposes, you can trace the intermediate steps:\n\nres, steps = simplify(expr, alg_rules; mode=:trace)\n\nfor step in steps\n    println(\"Before: \", step.before)\n    println(\"After:  \", step.after)\n    println(\"Rule used: \", step.rule.name)\nend\n\nRules loaded from JSON retain their stable OSR name (identity:id) and their description. For streaming output, logging, or a graphical interface, pass a callback instead of relying on the library to print:\n\nsimplify(expr, alg_rules; on_step=step -> @info \"rewrite\" rule=step.rule.name)","title":"Step-by-Step Tracing"},{"category":"section","location":"10-tutorials/10-basic-usage/#Loading-an-OSR-rule-set-profile","page":"Basic Usage","text":"@load_osr_profile compiles the ordered manifest of a rule-set repository. The repository can be a sibling checkout of this package or any local clone with a rules/meta.json manifest:\n\ncalculus_rules = @load_osr_profile(\"../../Calculus\")\n\nThe package provides the OpenMath heads used by the current Calculus profile, including circular, hyperbolic, and inverse functions. No caller-side symbolic declarations are required merely to load that profile.","title":"Loading an OSR rule-set profile"},{"category":"section","location":"10-tutorials/10-basic-usage/#Piecewise-expressions","page":"Basic Usage","text":"A rewrite that is only valid on part of a domain keeps its validity conditions instead of discarding them. Piecewise holds a list of branches, each a Piece carrying a value and its condition, optionally closed by an Otherwise:\n\n[\"Piecewise\", [\"List\",\n  [\"Piece\", \"x_\", [\"IsPositive\", \"x_\"]],\n  [\"Otherwise\", [\"Multiply\", -1, \"x_\"]]\n]]\n\n@syms x\nabsolute = Piecewise([Piece(x, IsPositive(x)), Otherwise(Multiply(-1, x))])\npiecewise_pieces(absolute) # the branches, with `nothing` as the Otherwise condition\n\nselect_piece reduces a piecewise to the value of the branch that applies. Branches are examined in order, and a branch is taken once its condition is decided true and every earlier one is decided false. An undecided condition stops the search, because a later branch may not overtake one that might yet apply:\n\nselect_piece(absolute)                                  # unchanged: nothing is known about x\nassuming(IsPositive(x)) do select_piece(absolute) end   # x\n\ndecide_condition is the three-valued judgement behind it: true, false, or nothing when undecided. A condition is decided when it is a Boolean literal, when it is a relation over closed arithmetic expressions, or when it is an active hypothesis; And, Or, and Not combine those answers, so a conjunction with one false operand is false even when the other is unknown.\n\nA symbolic predicate answering false means \"not proved\", which is why an unproved condition leaves the piecewise intact rather than skipping the branch.","title":"Piecewise expressions"},{"category":"section","location":"10-tutorials/10-basic-usage/#Proving-an-equivalence","page":"Basic Usage","text":"prove searches for a rewrite path between two expressions and returns the proof that records it:\n\n@syms x\nrules = @load_osr_profile(\"path/to/Trigonometry\")\n\nproof = prove(Add(Power(Sin(x), 2), Power(Cos(x), 2)), 1, rules)\nverified(proof) # true\n\nOSRProof: verified\n  left:  Add(Power(Sin(x), 2), Power(Cos(x), 2))\n    Add(Power(Sin(x), 2), Power(Cos(x), 2))  =  1   [1.1:1] Pythagorean Identity\n    ⇒ 1\n  right: 1\n    ⇒ 1\n  both sides reduce to 1\n\nAn equation written with ~ can be proved directly, and hypotheses are carried into the proof:\n\nprove(Add(Power(Sin(x), 2), Power(Cos(x), 2)) ~ 1, rules)\nprove(Power(x, 0), 1, power_rules; assumptions=[IsNonzero(x)])\n\nBoth sides are normalised with the rule set and the normal forms are compared up to a renaming of bound variables, so the search succeeds exactly when the two sides are joinable. Each recorded step is local, which is what a rewrite tactic in a proof assistant consumes; every OSR rule is an oriented instance of an equation, so the steps on the right read backwards complete the derivation.\n\nThe search is sound but incomplete. A verified proof means every step is a rule application whose constraints held where it fired. A failure means the rule set offered no path, which is not a proof of inequivalence — a rule set that is not confluent can fail to join two equivalent expressions — so the result reads \"not proved\" rather than \"unequal\".","title":"Proving an equivalence"},{"category":"section","location":"10-tutorials/10-basic-usage/#Assumptions-and-Safe-Rewrites","page":"Basic Usage","text":"Rules carrying a predicate are applied only when it is established.  For example, NonzeroQ is mapped to is_nonzero: it accepts a nonzero literal, or a symbolic term explicitly declared nonzero.  This prevents the invalid unconditional rewrite 0^0 = 1.  Likewise, NotEqual does not infer a mathematical inequality from two structurally different symbolic terms.\n\n@syms x Power(a, b)\npower_rules = @load_osr(\"path/to/1.1-basic-exponents.json\")\n\nsimplify(Power(x, 0), power_rules)                         # unchanged\nsimplify(Power(x, 0), power_rules; assumptions=[IsNonzero(x)]) # 1","title":"Assumptions and Safe Rewrites"},{"category":"section","location":"10-tutorials/10-basic-usage/#What-a-hypothesis-establishes","page":"Basic Usage","text":"A hypothesis proves every property its domain implies, not only the one it was written as. Membership can be declared with a DomainSets.jl domain, an IntervalSets.jl interval, or a Julia type:\n\nassuming(x ∈ 2..5) do\n    is_positive(x) # true\n    is_nonzero(x)  # true\n    is_real(x)     # true\n    is_integer(x)  # false — an interval of reals proves nothing about integrality\nend\n\nassuming(x ∈ Integers()) do\n    is_integer(x) # true\n    is_real(x)    # true\nend\n\nA bound at zero only excludes zero when it is open, so x ∈ 0..Inf is not a proof of positivity while x ∈ OpenInterval(0, Inf) is. The relational hypotheses IsPositive, IsNegative, IsNonzero, IsInteger, GreaterThan, and LessThan work the same way: GreaterThan(x, 5) proves positivity, GreaterThan(x, -3) only proves that x is real.\n\nEach hypothesis is weighed on its own, so the context is sound but does not combine two hypotheses into a third: x ∈ ℤ proves integrality and x > 0 proves positivity, yet neither alone proves that x is a positive integer.\n\nWhen Symbolics.jl is loaded, its ∈ builds a VarDomainPairing instead of the package's ElementOf. Such a hypothesis is read as an OSR fact on entry, so assuming(x ∈ 2..5) behaves the same either way.","title":"What a hypothesis establishes"},{"category":"section","location":"10-tutorials/10-basic-usage/#Wildcards","page":"Basic Usage","text":"A rule's pattern declares its wildcards; its result and its constraints refer to the bindings the pattern made.\n\nSpelling Meaning Compiles to\n~x slot ~x\nx_ blank ~x\nx_integer typed blank ~x::IntegerQ\nxs__ sequence of at least one expression ~~xs, guarded non-empty\nxs___ sequence, possibly empty ~~xs\n\nA typed blank accepts the domains integer, rational, real, complex, and number; any other domain is a rule-file error rather than a silently inert rule.\n\nOSR also spells an optional operand a., as in RUBI's (a_. + b_.*x_)^m_. Matching one requires knowing the identity element of the enclosing operation, which SymbolicUtils provides only for the native +, *, and ^, never for the uninterpreted heads a rule file declares. The loader therefore rejects such a wildcard instead of compiling it into a rule that could never fire.","title":"Wildcards"},{"category":"section","location":"10-tutorials/10-basic-usage/#The-constraint-predicate-library","page":"Basic Usage","text":"A rule's constraints array is compiled into a Julia guard. Each entry names a predicate and applies it to OSR expressions:\n\n\"constraints\": [\n  [\"FreeQ\", \"~m\", \"x\"],\n  [\"Not\", [\"EqQ\", \"~m\", -1]],\n  [\"Or\", [\"IntegerQ\", \"~m\"], [\"And\", [\"GtQ\", \"~a\", 0], [\"GtQ\", \"~c\", 0]]]\n]\n\nNot, And, and Or are combinators: they nest constraints and compile to Julia control flow, never to a symbolic Not/And/Or term. Every other entry is a predicate application, and all entries of the array must hold.\n\nThe library implements the predicates below, covering 97% of the constraint applications in the RUBI dataset:\n\nGroup Predicates\nComparison EqQ, NeQ, GtQ, LtQ, GeQ, LeQ\nInteger-qualified IntegerQ, IntegersQ, IGtQ, ILtQ, IGeQ, ILeQ\nNumeric domain RationalQ, FractionQ, HalfIntegerQ, PosQ, NegQ, FalseQ, NumericQ, RealQ, ComplexQ\nStructural FreeQ, AtomQ, SumQ, ProductQ, PowerQ, MemberQ\n\nFreeQ accepts a collection on either side. A quantifier binds a list, so a side condition about its scope can ask about the whole binder, and RUBI's FreeQ[{a, b, m}, x] spelling asks about every element of a list. | Polynomial | PolynomialQ, PolyQ, LinearQ, QuadraticQ |\n\nGtQ, LtQ, GeQ, and LeQ accept RUBI's chained form, so GtQ(u, v, w) means u > v > w.\n\nEvery predicate is conservative: it answers true only when the property is established, so an unproved guard leaves its rewrite unapplied rather than risking an invalid one. A closed arithmetic expression is evaluated exactly, which is what makes a guard such as [\"PosQ\", [\"Power\", 2, -1]] decidable:\n\nOpenSymbolicRules.osr_number(Power(2, -1)) # 1//2\n\nFolding only recognises the canonical Add, Multiply, Subtract, Divide, and Power heads. A rule file that renames them still loads, but its arithmetic becomes opaque and its guarded rewrites stay inactive.\n\nA predicate the library does not define is resolved in the module that loads the rule file, so a host can supply its own:\n\nmodule Host\n    using OpenSymbolicRules\n    EvenIntegerQ(value) = value isa Integer && iseven(value)\n    const rules = @load_osr(\"path/to/rules.json\") # may use [\"EvenIntegerQ\", \"~m\"]\nend","title":"The constraint predicate library"},{"category":"section","location":"10-tutorials/10-basic-usage/#Symbolics.jl-Interoperability","page":"Basic Usage","text":"When Symbolics.jl is loaded, to_osr converts its differential operator into the canonical OpenMath-aligned representation Derivative(Lambda(x, f)). to_symbolics performs the inverse conversion, preserving the differentiation variable and expression.","title":"Symbolics.jl Interoperability"},{"category":"section","location":"10-tutorials/10-basic-usage/#Loading-a-Profile","page":"Basic Usage","text":"An OSR repository can expose an ordered default profile and named rewrite profiles in rules/meta.json:\n\nrules = @load_osr_profile(\"path/to/Logic\")\ncnf_rules = @load_osr_profile(\"path/to/Logic\", :to_cnf)\n\nMulti-premise inference profiles are read as structured data, leaving proof search and clause management to the host engine:\n\ninferences = load_inference_profile(\"path/to/Logic\", :resolution)\n\nLogic profiles use the exported canonical heads And, Or, Not, Implies, Equivalent, Forall, and Exists.\n\nQuantifier heads accept a vector of bound variable names.  A rule declaration using xs__ captures and preserves the complete vector, so a first-order rewrite can handle one or many bound variables without treating that vector as a scalar expression:\n\n@syms p\nrules = @load_osr(\"path/to/6.1-negation.json\")\nsimplify(Not(Forall([:x, :y], p)), rules) # Exists([:x, :y], Not(p))","title":"Loading a Profile"},{"category":"section","location":"10-tutorials/10-basic-usage/#Rule-dispatch","page":"Basic Usage","text":"simplify does not try every rule on every term. Rules are indexed by the operation their pattern requires at the root of a term, so applying a rule set costs one dictionary lookup rather than one matcher call per rule. On a synthetic 6000-rule set this applies rules roughly 470 times faster than a linear scan, which is what makes a catalogue the size of RUBI usable.\n\nThree kinds of pattern have no root requirement and are therefore always tried: a bare slot (~a), a slot in head position ((~f)(~a)), and a pattern holding an optional slot ((~a)^(~!b)), which SymbolicUtils lets match a term that lacks the operation entirely.\n\nDispatch is an optimisation, not a change of semantics: the rules that can match are applied in their original order, exactly as a linear scan would.","title":"Rule dispatch"},{"category":"section","location":"10-tutorials/10-basic-usage/#Binders-and-lexical-scope","page":"Basic Usage","text":"Lambda, Forall, and Exists introduce a lexical scope. Every other binding construct — an integral, a sum, a product, a derivative — carries its bound variable inside a Lambda, as the OpenMath fns1#lambda symbol prescribes, so the Calculus profile writes a derivative as Derivative(Lambda(x, body)):\n\n@syms x c\nrules = @load_osr_profile(\"path/to/Calculus\")\n\nsimplify(Derivative(Lambda(x, c)), rules)             # Lambda(x, 0)\nsimplify(Derivative(Lambda(x, Power(x, 2))), rules)   # Lambda(x, Multiply(2, Power(x, Add(2, -1))))\n\nA bound occurrence is not an occurrence of the free variable, and FreeQ respects that:\n\nFreeQ(Lambda(x, Sin(x)), x) # true\nfree_variables(Lambda(x, Add(x, y))) # Set([:y])\n\nosr_substitute substitutes for the free occurrences of a variable and is capture-avoiding: a binder whose variable occurs free in the replacement is alpha-renamed first, so the replacement keeps referring to the same variable it did outside the binder.\n\nosr_substitute(Lambda(y, Add(x, y)), x => y) # Lambda(y1, Add(y, y1))\n\nTwo expressions that differ only in the names of their bound variables are compared with alpha_equivalent:\n\nalpha_equivalent(Lambda(x, Sin(x)), Lambda(y, Sin(y))) # true\nalpha_equivalent(Lambda(x, Sin(x)), Lambda(y, Sin(x))) # false","title":"Binders and lexical scope"},{"category":"section","location":"10-tutorials/10-basic-usage/#Associativity-and-commutativity","page":"Basic Usage","text":"Associativity and commutativity are properties of the OpenMath symbol a head is bound to, never of the head's spelling. A rule file declares those bindings in its semantics block, so the file below gets commutative matching for Plus without having to be named Add:\n\n{\n  \"semantics\": { \"Plus\": \"openmath:arith1#plus\" },\n  \"rules\": [\n    { \"id\": 1, \"pattern\": [\"Plus\", \"~x\", 0], \"constraints\": [], \"result\": \"~x\" }\n  ]\n}\n\nAn n-ary expression headed by an associative symbol (arith1#plus, arith1#times, logic1#and, logic1#or, logic1#xor) is normalized to left-associated binary SymbolicUtils terms at load time.\n\nA pattern headed by a commutative symbol (arith1#plus and the Boolean connectives logic1#and, logic1#or, logic1#xor, logic1#xnor, logic1#nand, logic1#nor, logic1#equivalent) compiles to a SymbolicUtils.ACRule, which matches every operand order with a single rule. A rule file therefore never needs a mirrored copy of a commutative pattern:\n\nsimplify(Plus(x, 0), rules) # x\nsimplify(Plus(0, x), rules) # x\n\narith1#times is deliberately excluded. An OSR expression carries no shape information, so a times operand may be a matrix and reordering it would be unsound.\n\nWith Symbolics.jl, symbolic arrays can be declared with @variables A[1:m, 1:n]. Matrix addition may use Add when dimensions are compatible, but matrix multiplication must retain its operand order. A future matrix profile will need explicit shape constraints and matrix-product semantics rather than scalar arith1#times assumptions.\n\nThe same rule is stricter for tensors: addition is commutative only for equal shapes, whereas tensor product, contraction, and axis permutation are ordered operations. They must be represented by dedicated heads with explicit index and shape metadata; they must never be silently encoded as Multiply.","title":"Associativity and commutativity"},{"category":"section","location":"10-tutorials/10-basic-usage/#Exact-polynomial-normal-forms","page":"Basic Usage","text":"SparsePolynomial is an opt-in exact backend for algorithms over multivariate polynomials with rational coefficients. It is intentionally separate from general symbolic expressions: callers supply the polynomial ring variables and its sparse monomial map explicitly.\n\np = SparsePolynomial([:x, :y], Dict((2, 0) => 1, (0, 1) => 1)) # x² + y\nq = SparsePolynomial([:x, :y], Dict((1, 1) => 1, (0, 0) => -1)) # xy - 1\n\nspoly(p, q; ordering=:lex)              # y² + x\nnormal_form(p, [q]; ordering=:grevlex)  # normal form modulo q\ng = groebner_basis([p, q]; ordering=:lex)\nideal_membership(p, g; ordering=:lex)   # true\n\nThe available orders are :lex, :grlex, and :grevlex. This foundation is for exact algebraic algorithms; conversion from SymbolicUtils expressions and equation solving remain separate planned layers. For implementation inspection, low-level leading-term and S-polynomial primitives stay qualified as OpenSymbolicRules.leading_monomial and OpenSymbolicRules.spoly.\n\nAn explicit boundary retains the exact coefficient domain when exchanging polynomials with SymbolicUtils. The requested variables define the ring; functions, negative powers, and undeclared parameters are rejected.\n\nusing SymbolicUtils\n@syms x y\n\np = to_sparse_polynomial(x^2 * y + 3 // 2, [:x, :y])\nto_symbolic_polynomial(p, Dict(:x => x, :y => y))","title":"Exact polynomial normal forms"},{"category":"section","location":"10-tutorials/10-basic-usage/#Propositional-satisfiability","page":"Basic Usage","text":"The Boolean kernel includes a small pure-Julia DPLL solver for finite CNF problems. It has no native-library or solver-package dependency. Clauses use the standard DIMACS literal convention: a positive integer denotes a variable, and its negative denotes the variable's negation.\n\n# (a ∨ b) ∧ (¬a ∨ b) ∧ (a ∨ ¬b)\nsatisfiable([[1, 2], [-1, 2], [1, -2]]) # true\n\n# a ∧ ¬a\nsatisfiable([[1], [-1]])                # false\n\nThis is the Boolean component of a planned pure-Julia DPLL(T) architecture. Theory reasoning, such as equality and rational linear arithmetic, is added only when its result can retain explicit assumptions and proof obligations.\n\nFor a satisfiable CNF problem, sat_model returns a dictionary that witnesses the decision and can be checked directly against every clause. It returns nothing for an unsatisfiable formula.\n\nsat_model([[1, 2], [-1, 2]]) # Dict(1 => true, 2 => true)","title":"Propositional satisfiability"},{"category":"section","location":"10-tutorials/10-basic-usage/#Exact-rational-linear-theory","page":"Basic Usage","text":"linear_satisfiable decides a conjunction of linear constraints over ℚ using exact Fourier–Motzkin elimination. A constraint uses :le, :lt, or :eq for ≤, <, or = respectively.\n\nx_at_least_one = LinearConstraint(Dict(:x => -1), :le, -1)\nx_less_than_one = LinearConstraint(Dict(:x => 1), :lt, 1)\n\nlinear_satisfiable([x_at_least_one, x_less_than_one]) # false\n\nIt is the exact rational theory component used by the DPLL(T) integration below.\n\nFor a feasible system, linear_model reconstructs rational variable values from the elimination layers. Its result is an ordinary dictionary and can be checked against the original constraints without numerical tolerances.\n\nlinear_model([x_at_least_one]) # Dict(:x => 1)","title":"Exact rational linear theory"},{"category":"section","location":"10-tutorials/10-basic-usage/#Combining-Boolean-and-linear-constraints","page":"Basic Usage","text":"linear_smt_satisfiable now joins the local DPLL engine to linear theory atoms. CNF clauses refer to positive atom numbers; a negative literal is the exact logical negation of a non-strict or strict inequality.\n\natoms = Dict(\n    1 => LinearConstraint(Dict(:x => 1), :le, 0),\n    2 => LinearConstraint(Dict(:x => -1), :lt, -1), # x > 1\n)\n\nlinear_smt_satisfiable([[1, 2]], atoms) # x ≤ 0 ∨ x > 1; true\nlinear_smt_satisfiable([[1], [2]], atoms) # false\n\nlinear_smt_model returns both the Boolean choices and the rational assignment for the selected theory branch.\n\nmodel = linear_smt_model([[1, 2]], atoms)\nmodel.booleans\nmodel.rationals\n\nNegated equalities are expanded exactly as a < b ∨ a > b; the solver keeps those alternatives as explicit theory branches. Proof-producing explanations remain the next SMT milestone.","title":"Combining Boolean and linear constraints"},{"category":"section","location":"10-tutorials/10-basic-usage/#Equality-theory","page":"Basic Usage","text":"For ground, uninterpreted symbols, smt_satisfiable uses union-find to close equalities transitively before checking disequalities.\n\natoms = Dict(\n    1 => EqualityConstraint(:a, :b, :eq),\n    2 => EqualityConstraint(:a, :b, :ne),\n)\n\nsmt_satisfiable([[1, 2]], atoms) # true\nsmt_satisfiable([[1], [2]], atoms) # false\n\nsmt_model returns the Boolean choices and a map from every ground term to its equivalence-class representative.\n\nmodel = smt_model([[1, 2]], atoms)\nmodel.classes[:a] == model.classes[:b]\n\nThis equality theory is separate from rational linear arithmetic for now. For symbols interpreted as rational variables, use the explicitly sorted rational_smt_satisfiable entry point instead: it combines equality, disequality, and linear arithmetic by translating x = y into x - y = 0. It is intentionally distinct from the uninterpreted equality solver above.\n\nnumeric_atoms = Dict{Int,Union{LinearConstraint,EqualityConstraint}}(\n    1 => EqualityConstraint(:x, :y, :eq),\n    2 => LinearConstraint(Dict(:x => 1), :le, 0),\n    3 => LinearConstraint(Dict(:y => -1), :le, -1),\n)\n\nrational_smt_satisfiable([[1], [2], [3]], numeric_atoms) # false","title":"Equality theory"},{"category":"section","location":"10-tutorials/10-basic-usage/#Checking-assumption-consistency","page":"Basic Usage","text":"The same exact rational engine can reject contradictory CAS hypotheses before they are used to justify a conditional rewrite. It reports nothing rather than guessing when a fact is outside its supported fragment.\n\n@syms x\nrational_assumptions_satisfiable([GreaterThan(x, 0), LessThan(x, 0)]) # false\nrational_assumptions_satisfiable([IsNonzero(x)]) # nothing\n\nExact interval memberships participate in the same check, including open and closed endpoints.\n\nrational_assumptions_satisfiable([x ∈ 1..2, LessThan(x, 1)]) # false","title":"Checking assumption consistency"},{"category":"section","location":"#OpenSymbolicRules.jl","page":"OpenSymbolicRules.jl","text":"Documentation for OpenSymbolicRules.jl.\n\nOpenSymbolicRules.jl is a zero-overhead, highly pedagogical rule engine client for the language-agnostic Open Symbolic Rules JSON format. It integrates directly with SymbolicUtils.jl to provide both blazingly fast simplifications and step-by-step educational tracing.","title":"OpenSymbolicRules.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Features","page":"OpenSymbolicRules.jl","text":"AOT Compilation: Parses JSON rules at macro expansion time (@load_osr) to emit pure Julia code with zero runtime overhead.\nUnified Engine: Combines rules from different domains (Algebra, Trigonometry, Calculus) into a single rewrite engine.\nObservable Tracing: mode=:trace records every rewrite with its stable OSR rule name; on_step can send those events to a notebook, logger, or user interface.","title":"Features"}]
}
